  "lesson_1_key": "A prefix is a shortcut for a long address. Every prefix you use must be declared at the top with an @prefix … . line, otherwise you get an error.",
  "@lesson_1_key": {
    "description": "Lesson 1 «Triple and prefix»: the key point in one sentence",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_1_note_0": "<b>The full stop at the end</b> of every description is mandatory. A forgotten full stop or a \";\" instead of \".\" is the most common error in Turtle.",
  "@lesson_1_note_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 1 «Triple and prefix»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_1_note_1": "<b>The prefix name is your choice</b> (you can use <code>b:</code> instead of <code>:</code>); only the IRI it points to matters. But <code>:SpotH</code> and <code>b:SpotH</code> are the same only if both prefixes point to the same address.",
  "@lesson_1_note_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 1 «Triple and prefix»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_1_note_2": "<code>&lt;test#SpotH&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;https://example.org/bivak#SpotH&gt;</code> are <b>different things</b> even if named alike. Address = name of the thing. In the task the prefix <code>:</code> must be exactly bivak.",
  "@lesson_1_note_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 1 «Triple and prefix»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_1_note_3": "The empty prefix <code>:</code> is a prefix like any other; it must be declared too. The task always gives your own prefixes (<code>:</code>, <code>bv:</code>, <code>kat:</code>) in full; the standard ones (<code>rdf rdfs xsd owl skos rml</code>) are in the right-hand panel.",
