  "lesson_1_theory": "<p>All data in RDF is made of short \"sentences\" with three parts: <mark>who/what</mark> (subject), <mark>which property</mark> (predicate), <mark>equal to what</mark> (object). A full stop ends the sentence.</p>\n<pre>:SpotH   rdfs:label   \"Louka\"@cs .\n# subject  predicate    object</pre>\n<h4>Why a prefix?</h4>\n<p>Every thing has a <b>full address</b> (an IRI). Without abbreviations the triple looks like this &mdash; long and clumsy:</p>\n<pre>&lt;https://example.org/bivak#SpotH&gt; &lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label&gt; \"Louka\"@cs .</pre>\n<p>A <mark>prefix is a shortcut</mark> for the beginning of an address. You declare once \"let <code>rdfs:</code> mean <code>http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#</code>\" and then write it short:</p>\n<pre>@prefix : &lt;https://example.org/bivak#&gt; .\n@prefix rdfs: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#&gt; .\n\n:SpotH rdfs:label \"Louka\"@cs .</pre>\n<p>The parser substitutes: <code>:SpotH</code> &rarr; <code>&lt;https://example.org/bivak#SpotH&gt;</code>, <code>rdfs:label</code> &rarr; <code>&lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label&gt;</code>. Same data.</p>\n<h4>Where terms come from</h4>\n<p><code>rdfs:label</code> is \"name\" from the <b>RDFS</b> vocabulary (RDF Schema, a W3C standard): that is the <code>rdfs:</code> prefix. Our own vocabulary here is <code>:</code> (the bivak dataset); <code>:SpotH</code> is something we invented, <code>rdfs:label</code> is a common standard.</p>\n<h4>What is <code>https://example.org/bivak#</code>?</h4>\n<p>Just a <mark>unique name</mark>, not a link to a website. <code>example.org</code> is a domain reserved for examples; <code>bivak</code> is the dataset name; <code>#</code> is a separator, the name of the thing follows it. Names are built from a domain owned by the author so they are unique worldwide. In seminar 6 the address <code>https://kbss.fel.cvut.cz/bivak#</code> is the same idea built from the department's real domain.</p>\n<h4>The same text without prefixes, and mixed</h4>\n<pre># no prefixes at all:\n&lt;https://example.org/bivak#SpotH&gt; &lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label&gt; \"Louka\"@cs .\n\n# mixed: prefix only for label\n@prefix rdfs: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#&gt; .\n&lt;https://example.org/bivak#SpotH&gt; rdfs:label \"Louka\"@cs .</pre>\n<p><code>@cs</code> after a string is the language tag.</p>",
