  },
  "lesson_20_key": "CONSTRUCT = a SELECT that returns a graph instead of a table.",
  "@lesson_20_key": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: the key point in one sentence",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_20_note_0": "The property in CONSTRUCT (for example <code>:inForest</code>) <b>can be invented</b> &mdash; it appears only in the result; the source data need not contain it.",
  "@lesson_20_note_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_20_note_1": "Variables in the CONSTRUCT template must be bound in WHERE: otherwise the triple is not built.",
  "@lesson_20_note_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_20_note_2": "The result of CONSTRUCT can be saved as a new Turtle file or inserted into the data with INSERT.",
  "@lesson_20_note_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_20_task": "Use CONSTRUCT to build triples <code>?stay :inForest ?forest</code> for stays whose spot lies (directly or indirectly) in a forest.",
  "@lesson_20_task": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: task statement",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_20_term_0": "",
  "@lesson_20_term_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: short explanation of the English term «CONSTRUCT query» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_20_term_1": "",
  "@lesson_20_term_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: short explanation of the English term «graph template» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_20_term_2": "",
  "@lesson_20_term_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: short explanation of the English term «new triples» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_20_theory": "<p>Instead of a table, <code>CONSTRUCT</code> <mark>builds new triples</mark>. The result template is on top, WHERE says what to look for:</p>\n<pre>CONSTRUCT &#123; ?spot :inProtectedArea ?a &#125;       # what to build\nWHERE &#123;\n  ?spot :liesWithin+ ?a .                     # where to take it from\n  ?a a/rdfs:subClassOf* :ProtectedArea .\n&#125;</pre>\n<p>The result is a graph (a set of triples) like:</p>\n<pre>:SpotA :inProtectedArea :NP_CeskeSvycarsko .\n:SpotB :inProtectedArea :NPR_KokorinskyDul .\n:SpotB :inProtectedArea :CHKO_Kokorinsko .\n...</pre>\n<p>The CONSTRUCT template may use any variables from WHERE and <b>any new property</b> (here <code>:inProtectedArea</code> does not exist in the data at all). For the task you need the same idea with a different relation.</p>",
  "@lesson_20_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_20_title": "CONSTRUCT",
  "@lesson_20_title": {
    "description": "Lesson 20 «CONSTRUCT»: title"
  },
  "lesson_21_hint_0": "Simply: <code>?area a :ProtectedArea ; rdfs:label ?label .</code> + the cs filter. And turn the checkbox on.",
  "@lesson_21_hint_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: hint 1",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_21_key": "Include inferred ON = inferred triples are visible too (for example a national park is \"automatically\" a ProtectedArea).",
  "@lesson_21_key": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: the key point in one sentence",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_21_note_0": "Inference <b>adds rows</b>: results with and without it differ. On the test check whether \"Include inferred\" is on if the answer is not what you expect.",
  "@lesson_21_note_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_21_note_1": "Paths (<code>a/rdfs:subClassOf*</code>, <code>:liesWithin+</code>) work <b>without</b> inference. That is why tasks often ask for paths.",
  "@lesson_21_note_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_21_note_2": "Inference derives types from domain/range <b>without checking</b> the data: a typo in the data produces a false type. Data validation is separate (SHACL, only mentioned in the materials).",
  "@lesson_21_note_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_21_origin_0": "subclass",
  "@lesson_21_origin_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: what the term rdfs:subClassOf (RDF Schema) means, a few words"
  },
  "lesson_21_origin_1": "type of the property's subject",
  "@lesson_21_origin_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: what the term rdfs:domain (RDF Schema) means, a few words"
  },
  "lesson_21_origin_2": "type of the property's value",
  "@lesson_21_origin_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: what the term rdfs:range (RDF Schema) means, a few words"
  },
  "lesson_21_task": "<b>Turn on \"Include inferred\"</b> and list all <code>:ProtectedArea</code> areas with their Czech labels. Columns: <code>?area</code>, <code>?label</code>. (8 rows.) Try it with inference off first — the result is empty. <div class=\"small\"><b>An area</b> is a thing of type <code>:Area</code> or one of its subclasses: a national park, CHKO, NPR or forest (for example <code>:NP_Sumava</code>, <code>:CHKO_Kokorinsko</code>, <code>:Forest_Pelhrimov</code>). A spot is linked to it by <code>:liesWithin</code>: <code>:SpotA :liesWithin :NP_CeskeSvycarsko</code>. A spot that \"lies in no area\" simply has no <code>:liesWithin</code> triple at all.</div>",
  "@lesson_21_task": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: task statement",
    "context": "html"
  },
