  },
  "lesson_21_theory": "<p>RDFS can <mark>infer new triples</mark> from rules:</p>\n<pre>x has type A, A is a subclass of B    &rArr;  x has type B       (rdfs:subClassOf)\nx :p y, :p has domain D              &rArr;  x has type D       (rdfs:domain)\nx :p y, :p has range R               &rArr;  y has type R       (rdfs:range)</pre>\n<p><b>RDFS</b> (RDF Schema) is a standard W3C vocabulary, prefix <code>rdfs:</code>: <code>rdfs:subClassOf</code>, <code>rdfs:domain</code>, <code>rdfs:range</code> all come from it.</p>\n<p>Example. The data has: <code>:NP_Sumava a :NationalPark .</code>, <code>:NationalPark rdfs:subClassOf :ProtectedArea .</code> and <code>:ProtectedArea rdfs:subClassOf :Area .</code></p>\n<pre>query:  SELECT ?a WHERE &#123; ?a a :Area &#125;\n\nwithout inference:  0 rows   (nobody is declared :Area directly)\nwith inference:     10 rows  (4 parks, 3 CHKO, NPR and 2 forests &mdash; along the subclass chain)</pre>\n<p>In GraphDB this is the <b>Include inferred</b> switch; here it is the checkbox above the editor.</p>\n<p>Side effect: for <code>:usesShelter [ a :Tarp ]</code> inference also adds the type <code>:Shelter</code> (from <code>rdfs:range</code>), so <code>?shelter a ?type</code> returns more rows: 12 instead of 6 in GraphDB (even more in this trainer, which applies the general RDFS rules).</p>",
  "@lesson_21_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 21 «Inference: what RDFS adds»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
