  "lesson_22_theory": "<p>A data schema is also written with triples. Everything highlighted below is from the <b>RDFS</b> vocabulary (<code>rdfs:</code>), except <code>rdf:Property</code> from the <b>RDF</b> vocabulary:</p>\n<pre>:Bridge a rdfs:Class ;                  # a new class\n    rdfs:subClassOf :Spot ;             # Bridge is a kind of spot (subclass)\n    rdfs:label \"Most\"@cs , \"Bridge\"@en .  # names\n\n:lengthM a rdf:Property ;               # a property\n    rdfs:domain :Bridge ;               # who can have it (type of the subject)\n    rdfs:range  xsd:integer .           # what the value is (xsd = XSD vocabulary)</pre>\n<p>For a relation property the range is another class; for a value property it is a datatype:</p>\n<pre>:atSpot  a rdf:Property ; rdfs:range :Spot .          # the value is a thing of class Spot\n:areaHa  a rdf:Property ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal .    # the value is a decimal number</pre>\n<p>The fact <code>:Bridge1 :lengthM 40 .</code> + this definition &rArr; with inference <code>:Bridge1 a :Bridge</code>, and <code>:Bridge1 a :Spot</code> (by subClassOf).</p>",
  "@lesson_22_theory": {
