  "lesson_25_theory": "<p>Some SKOS rules nobody checks for you &mdash; you check them with a query. Example: \"concepts of the category vocabulary with no mapping to an external vocabulary\" (<code>skos:exactMatch</code> = \"the same concept in another vocabulary\"):</p>\n<pre>SELECT ?notation\nWHERE &#123;\n  ?c skos:inScheme kat:schema ;          # concept in the kat vocabulary\n     skos:notation ?notation .\n  FILTER NOT EXISTS &#123; ?c skos:exactMatch ?x &#125;     # but no mapping\n&#125;</pre>\n<p>Result: <code>MZCHU</code>, <code>VZCHU</code>, <code>OP</code> &mdash; these are groups added by hand that have no counterpart in the external register. The other categories (NP, CHKO, NPR…) have an <code>exactMatch</code>.</p>\n<p>All terms (<code>skos:inScheme</code>, <code>skos:notation</code>, <code>skos:exactMatch</code>) come from the <b>SKOS</b> vocabulary. The same trick works for any \"something is missing\": only the property inside <code>NOT EXISTS</code> and the vocabulary change.</p>\n<p>Other checks from the seminar:</p>\n<pre># two main names in one language (violates a SKOS rule)\n?c skos:prefLabel ?l1 , ?l2 .\nFILTER (lang(?l1) = lang(?l2) &amp;&amp; STR(?l1) &lt; STR(?l2))\n\n# a value in the data that is not in the vocabulary\n?area ?p ?value .  FILTER NOT EXISTS &#123; ?value skos:inScheme ?s &#125;</pre>",
  "@lesson_25_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 25 «SKOS: vocabulary quality»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_25_title": "SKOS: vocabulary quality",
  "@lesson_25_title": {
    "description": "Lesson 25 «SKOS: vocabulary quality»: title"
  },
