  "lesson_26_theory": "<p>RML is a set of <mark>rules for turning a table into RDF</mark>. You describe <b>what</b> to produce; a program (Morph-KGC) reads the CSV itself. Each table row &rarr; one subject. All <code>rml:…</code> terms come from the <b>RML</b> vocabulary (prefix <code>rml:</code> = <code>http://w3id.org/rml/</code>).</p>\n<p>CSV (the relevant columns):</p>\n<pre>KOD,KAT,NAZEV,ROZL,...\n110,NPP,Hojná voda,9.1887,...\n112,NPP,Holý vrch,5.1854,...</pre>\n<p>Example rule: the <b>area code</b> (column KOD) as a plain string:</p>\n<pre>:Example a rml:TriplesMap ;                               # one rule\n  rml:logicalSource [ rml:source \"mzchu-sample.csv\" ;      # which file\n                      rml:referenceFormulation rml:CSV ] ;  # format &mdash; CSV\n  rml:subjectMap [ rml:template \"https://kbss.fel.cvut.cz/bivak#MZCHU_&#123;KOD&#125;\" ] ;   # subject address from column KOD\n  rml:predicateObjectMap [                                 # one \"property&ndash;value\" pair\n      rml:predicate bv:aopkCode ;                          # property\n      rml:objectMap [ rml:reference \"KOD\" ] ] .            # value &mdash; column KOD</pre>\n<p>Result for the first row:</p>\n<pre>bv:MZCHU_110  bv:aopkCode  \"110\" .</pre>\n<p>To add another property write another <code>rml:predicateObjectMap [ … ]</code> separated by <code>;</code>. For the task you need a different property and a different column; and also the value's <b>language</b> (<code>rml:language \"cs\"</code>).</p>",
  "@lesson_26_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 26 «RML: first mapping»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
