  "lesson_28_theory": "<p>A template makes an IRI by default. To make it produce <b>text</b>, add <code>rml:termType rml:Literal</code> (<code>rml:…</code> terms are from the <b>RML</b> vocabulary):</p>\n<pre>rml:objectMap [ rml:template \"Area: &#123;NAZEV&#125; (&#123;KAT&#125;)\" ;\n                rml:termType rml:Literal ;\n                rml:language \"cs\" ]                    # &rarr; \"Area: Hojná voda (NPP)\"@cs</pre>\n<p>To say \"this subject is of such a class\", add <code>rml:class</code> right in <code>subjectMap</code>:</p>\n<pre>rml:subjectMap [ rml:template \"…/bivak#MZCHU_&#123;KOD&#125;\" ;\n                 rml:class bv:Example ]                # &rarr; …MZCHU_110 a bv:Example</pre>\n<p>If a value must become an IRI but is a literal (or vice versa), set <code>rml:termType rml:IRI</code> / <code>rml:Literal</code>.</p>\n<p>One mapping = one <code>rml:TriplesMap</code>: a source, a subject (with a class) and several \"property&ndash;value\" pairs.</p>",
  "@lesson_28_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 28 «RML: template literal and subject class»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
