    "description": "Lesson 3 «Numbers, dates, booleans»: short explanation of the English term «decimal / integer / boolean / date» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_3_theory": "<p>The value after a property (the object) need not be the address of a thing; it can be a <mark>plain value</mark> &mdash; a literal: text, number, date. <b>You do not \"declare\" a number separately</b> &mdash; you just write it in the object position. The way you write it decides the type:</p>\n<table><tr><th>what you write</th><th>resulting type</th><th>type comes from</th></tr>\n<tr><td><code>9.1887</code></td><td>number with a dot &rarr; <code>xsd:decimal</code></td><td>XSD vocabulary (<code>xsd:</code>)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>5</code></td><td>integer &rarr; <code>xsd:integer</code></td><td>XSD</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>true</code> &nbsp; <code>false</code></td><td>yes / no (no quotes!) &rarr; <code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>XSD</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"2005-01-20\"^^xsd:date</code></td><td>date: in quotes, then <code>^^</code> and the type</td><td>XSD</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>\"110\"</code></td><td>string (in quotes, no <code>^^</code>) &rarr; <code>xsd:string</code></td><td>XSD</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>XSD</b> = XML Schema datatypes, a W3C standard; declare the prefix: <code>@prefix xsd: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#&gt; .</code> All datatypes (<code>xsd:decimal</code>, <code>xsd:integer</code>, <code>xsd:boolean</code>, <code>xsd:date</code>, <code>xsd:string</code>) live in this vocabulary.</p>\n<p>A full example on another thing &mdash; this is exactly what an answer looks like:</p>\n<pre>:Area7 :areaHa 12.5 ;                        # decimal\n       :visitors 340 ;                         # integer\n       :isOpen true ;                          # boolean\n       :opened \"1999-05-01\"^^xsd:date ;        # date\n       :code \"007\" .                           # string</pre>\n<p>Why types: with a type you can compare and sort (<code>FILTER (?d &gt;= \"2020-01-01\"^^xsd:date)</code>). Without a type a date is just text and such a query will not work.</p>",
  "@lesson_3_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 3 «Numbers, dates, booleans»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_3_title": "Numbers, dates, booleans",
