  "lesson_5_theory": "<p>SPARQL is a language for asking questions to a graph. You write a <mark>triple with an unknown part</mark> (a <code>?variable</code>) and the engine finds all matching values.</p>\n<pre>SELECT ?park                # what to show in the table\nWHERE &#123;                     # what to look for\n  ?park a :NationalPark .   # \"?park is a national park\"\n&#125;</pre>\n<p>Result on the data (4 rows): <code>:NP_Sumava</code>, <code>:NP_Krkonose</code>, <code>:NP_Podyji</code>, <code>:NP_CeskeSvycarsko</code>.</p>\n<p>Same idea, but the unknown stands in the <b>object</b> position. Which classes are direct subclasses of <code>:Area</code> (<code>rdfs:subClassOf</code> = \"subclass\", RDFS vocabulary)?</p>\n<pre>SELECT ?class\nWHERE &#123; ?class rdfs:subClassOf :Area . &#125;     # result: :ProtectedArea, :Forest</pre>\n<p>The recipe is always the same: <b>put the known parts into the pattern, replace the unknown with a ?variable</b>, and list the ones you want to see in SELECT. <code>a</code> is the same as <code>rdf:type</code> (RDF vocabulary).</p>",
  "@lesson_5_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 5 «SELECT and the triple pattern»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_5_title": "SELECT and the triple pattern",
  "@lesson_5_title": {
    "description": "Lesson 5 «SELECT and the triple pattern»: title"
  },
