  "lesson_78_task": "Open the schema (the editor already holds <code>four-thousanders-schema.ttl</code>) and extend it. <b>a)</b> A class <i>route</i> with the properties <i>route name</i> and <i>difficulty grade</i>, plus a property linking a summit to its normal route. Document every new class and property with SKOS like the rest: <code>skos:inScheme</code>, <code>skos:prefLabel</code>, <code>skos:definition</code>, <code>rdfs:domain</code>, <code>rdfs:range</code>. <b>b)</b> A separate SKOS concept scheme with the grades F, PD, AD, D, TD, ED: each with a <code>skos:notation</code> and a French <code>prefLabel</code> (facile, peu difficile, assez difficile, difficile, très difficile, extrêmement difficile). PD- and PD+ are narrower (<code>skos:narrower</code>) than PD. The difficulty-grade property from a) points to these concepts (domain: route). <div class=\"small\">Choose the IRI names yourself (the <code>uiaa-s:</code> prefix is already declared).</div>",
  "@lesson_78_task": {
    "description": "Lesson 78 «Task 3 – schema: route and difficulty»: task statement",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_78_title": "Task 3 – schema: route and difficulty",
