  "@lesson_7_term_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 7 «Several patterns and ;»: short explanation of the English term «join on a variable» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_7_term_2": "",
  "@lesson_7_term_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 7 «Several patterns and ;»: short explanation of the English term «DISTINCT» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_7_theory": "<p>You can list several triples in <code>WHERE</code> &mdash; only nodes for which <mark>all of them hold</mark> match. A shared variable links the triples. <code>;</code> works as in Turtle:</p>\n<pre>SELECT ?stay ?spot ?label\nWHERE &#123;\n  ?stay :atSpot ?spot ;          # the stay has a spot\n        rdfs:label ?label .      # and a name\n&#125;</pre>\n<p>This equals two separate triples sharing <code>?stay</code>:</p>\n<pre>WHERE &#123; ?stay :atSpot ?spot .  ?stay rdfs:label ?label . &#125;</pre>\n<p>Result: 12 rows &mdash; each of the 6 stays has two labels (cs and en):</p>\n<pre>:Stay1   :SpotA   \"Celta pod převisem\"@cs\n:Stay1   :SpotA   \"Tarp under the overhang\"@en\n:Stay2   :SpotB   \"Jen spacák v Kokořínském dole\"@cs\n...</pre>\n<p>The shared variable may be the <b>object</b> of one triple and the subject of another &mdash; this builds chains: <code>?stay :atSpot ?spot . ?spot :liesWithin ?area .</code></p>\n<h4>Repeated rows and DISTINCT</h4>\n<p>When triples are joined, the same thing can land in the result <b>several times</b>. Ask at which spots stays took place:</p>\n<pre>SELECT ?spot\nWHERE &#123; ?stay :atSpot ?spot . &#125;</pre>\n<p>Result &mdash; 6 rows, because <code>:SpotB</code> has two stays (<code>:Stay2</code> and <code>:Stay3</code>) and is printed twice:</p>\n<pre>:SpotA\n:SpotB\n:SpotB      &larr; repeat\n:SpotC\n:SpotD\n:SpotE</pre>\n<p><mark><code>DISTINCT</code> removes repeated rows</mark>. It is written right after <code>SELECT</code>:</p>\n<pre>SELECT DISTINCT ?spot\nWHERE &#123; ?stay :atSpot ?spot . &#125;       # 5 rows, :SpotB once</pre>\n<p>Whole <b>rows</b> are compared: if all selected columns are equal the duplicate is removed. If you also select <code>?stay</code> the rows differ (<code>:SpotB :Stay2</code> &ne; <code>:SpotB :Stay3</code>) and nothing is removed.</p>",
  "@lesson_7_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 7 «Several patterns and ;»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_7_title": "Several patterns and ;",
  "@lesson_7_title": {
    "description": "Lesson 7 «Several patterns and ;»: title"
  },
  "lesson_8_hint_0": "Inside the braces of <code>WHERE</code> add: <code>FILTER (lang(?label) = \"cs\")</code>",
  "@lesson_8_hint_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: hint 1",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_8_key": "FILTER goes inside &#123; &#125; and keeps only the matching rows.",
  "@lesson_8_key": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: the key point in one sentence",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_8_note_0": "<code>lang(?label) = \"cs\"</code> &mdash; the language is <b>in quotes</b> and without <code>@</code>. <code>lang(?label) = @cs</code> is an error.",
  "@lesson_8_note_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_8_note_1": "A string without a language (<code>\"Stan\"</code>) is <b>dropped</b> by <code>lang(…)=\"cs\"</code>. If labels have no language, no language filter is needed.",
  "@lesson_8_note_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_8_note_2": "Put FILTER <b>inside</b> the braces of WHERE, not after them.",
  "@lesson_8_note_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
