  "lesson_8_term_2": "",
  "@lesson_8_term_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: short explanation of the English term «language-tagged literal» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_8_theory": "<p><code>FILTER (condition)</code> throws away unwanted rows. For language-tagged strings, <code>lang(?x)</code> returns the tag: <code>\"cs\"</code>, <code>\"en\"</code>.</p>\n<pre>SELECT ?park ?name\nWHERE &#123;\n  ?park a :NationalPark ; rdfs:label ?name .\n  FILTER (lang(?name) = \"en\")        # keep only English names\n&#125;</pre>\n<p>Result: 4 parks, only <code>@en</code>: \"Bohemian Switzerland National Park\", \"Krkonoše National Park\", \"Podyjí National Park\", \"Šumava National Park\". Without FILTER there would be 8 rows (two labels per park).</p>\n<p>How literals differ:</p>\n<pre>\"Stan\"@cs        lang = \"cs\"\n\"Stan\"@en        lang = \"en\"\n\"Stan\"           lang = \"\"   (no language &mdash; not cs!)</pre>\n<p>You can invert the condition: <code>FILTER (lang(?name) != \"en\")</code> &mdash; every language except English.</p>",
  "@lesson_8_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_8_title": "FILTER and lang()",
  "@lesson_8_title": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 «FILTER and lang()»: title"
  },
  "lesson_9_hint_0": "A stay has <code>:atSpot ?spot</code> and <code>rdfs:label ?label</code>.",
  "@lesson_9_hint_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: hint 1",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_hint_1": "<code>FILTER (lang(?label) = \"cs\" && CONTAINS(?label, \"Kokoř\"))</code>",
  "@lesson_9_hint_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: hint 2",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_key": "Combine conditions in FILTER with && and ||; for text use CONTAINS, STRSTARTS, REGEX.",
  "@lesson_9_key": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: the key point in one sentence",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_note_0": "Functions (<code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>STRSTARTS</code>, <code>REGEX</code>, <code>lang</code>) are part of SPARQL; they need no prefixes.",
  "@lesson_9_note_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_note_1": "The parentheses of <code>FILTER ( … )</code> are mandatory, and inside the condition close every one: a common mistake is a missing <code>)</code>.",
  "@lesson_9_note_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_note_2": "<code>CONTAINS</code> is case-sensitive: \"kokoř\" will not find \"Kokoř\". For case-insensitive use <code>REGEX(?x, \"kokoř\", \"i\")</code>.",
  "@lesson_9_note_2": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: note box",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_task": "Find the stays whose Czech label contains \"Kokoř\". Columns: <code>?stay</code>, <code>?label</code>. (2 rows.)",
  "@lesson_9_task": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: task statement",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_term_0": "",
  "@lesson_9_term_0": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: short explanation of the English term «CONTAINS / STRSTARTS / REGEX» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_9_term_1": "",
  "@lesson_9_term_1": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: short explanation of the English term «logical AND (&&), OR (||), NOT (!)» (may stay empty)"
  },
  "lesson_9_theory": "<p>Conditions combine with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> (and), <code>||</code> (or), <code>!</code> (not). String functions:</p>\n<pre>CONTAINS(?x, \"park\")         # contains a substring\nSTRSTARTS(?x, \"CHKO\")        # starts with\nREGEX(?x, \"^Louka\", \"i\")     # regular expression; \"i\" = ignore case\nSTR(?iri)                    # an IRI as plain text</pre>\n<p>Examples on areas:</p>\n<pre># Czech names starting with \"CHKO\"   &rarr; 3 rows (Jizerské hory, Kokořínsko, Labské pískovce)\nFILTER (lang(?l) = \"cs\" &amp;&amp; STRSTARTS(?l, \"CHKO\"))\n\n# Czech names containing \"park\"      &rarr; 4 national parks\nFILTER (lang(?l) = \"cs\" &amp;&amp; CONTAINS(?l, \"park\"))\n\n# \"or\": CHKO or NPR\nFILTER (STRSTARTS(?l, \"CHKO\") || STRSTARTS(?l, \"NPR\"))\n\n# \"not\": everything except names containing \"park\"\nFILTER (!CONTAINS(?l, \"park\"))</pre>\n<p>The filter is applied to <b>every</b> row: make sure the variable (<code>?l</code>) is already defined in WHERE above it.</p>",
  "@lesson_9_theory": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 «FILTER with CONTAINS and &&»: theory",
    "context": "html"
  },
  "lesson_9_title": "FILTER with CONTAINS and &&",
